### Quest Lines ###

## GT Simple Wireless Network

betterquesting.questline.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAQ.name=§5§lGT Simple Wireless Network
betterquesting.questline.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAQ.desc=§3§lPurpose and operation§r%nGTSWN provides wireless EU transfer for supported GT energy containers, grid monitoring, physical information screens and device status management, with ME quantum bridging through AE2.%n%n§3§lChapter route§r%nThe §114§r quests cover the Wireless Energy Monitor, Portable Wireless Network Monitor, Link Terminal and covers, information screens, ME quantum networks and the Device Info Terminal. Start with a physical screen for EU trends, AE trends or AE inventory; carry its bound monitor to view the full screen remotely and select simplified entries for the HUD. The Device Info Terminal instead binds machines for an EU ledger, world labels, heat visualization and teleportation.%n%n§3§lOperating requirements§r%nKeep participating machine, screen and quantum-anchor chunks loaded. ME features require valid AE power and channel management. Crafting or item checks and manual checkboxes follow the individual quest requirements; reading a tutorial does not automatically verify its machine settings.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAQ.name=§2§lEverything Under Control
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAQ.desc=§3§lPurpose and operation§r%nGTSWN provides wireless EU transfer, wireless network monitoring, network info panels, ME quantum bridging and device status management. Wireless energy features serve supported GT energy containers; ME quantum features require AE2.%n%n§3§lChapter route§r%nThis chapter contains §114§r quests: start with the Wireless Energy Monitor and Portable Wireless Network Monitor, then configure the Link Terminal and its energy and power covers, and continue to info panels, the ME Quantum Terminal and the Device Info Terminal. Tick this quest after reading.%n%n§3§lOperating requirements§r%nThe relevant machines, panels and quantum anchors need their chunks loaded. ME bridging also requires channel capacity management and AE network power; material and power requirements vary by feature.%n%n§3§lChoose your monitor§r%nUse the Portable Wireless Network Monitor for the grid overview, battery charging and views of bound physical screens. Use the Device Info Terminal for individual machines, EU input/output, recipe previews and visualization. Their bindings and right-click gestures are separate; follow the relevant quest before configuring them.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAg.name=§3§lWatching the Watts
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAg.desc=§3§lPurpose and operation§r%nThe Wireless Energy Monitor reads wireless grid energy and power changes. It accepts and outputs no EU, stores no EU internally, and needs no power supply. Data updates every §1100 ticks (5 seconds)§r over a §1300-second§r history window.%n%n§3§lPreparation and quest§r%nThe crafting-table recipe requires §14§r LV Sensors, §12§r Steel Plates, §11§r LV Machine Hull and §12§r Steel Screws. This quest detects §11§r monitor and §2does not consume it§r.%n%n§3§lChunk loading§r%nKeep the monitor chunk loaded. If the gap between updates exceeds §1200 ticks (10 seconds)§r, the monitor clears trend samples and delays redstone checks by §1600 ticks (30 seconds)§r. Shorter gaps retain the samples. This handles unloading or gaps without updates, rather than loss of power.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAw.name=§3§lSmart Grid
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAw.desc=§3§lPreparation and use§r%nPlace the Wireless Energy Monitor and right-click to open its interface; no power connection is needed. The measurement anchor selects wireless stored EU, §15-second§r average EU/t or instantaneous EU/t. Parameters accept direct entry and scientific notation, such as §11.5E6§r.%n%n§3§lRedstone control§r%nThe redstone button offers §15§r modes: Off; output above Param1; output below Param1; High-Hysteresis (enable above Param1, disable below Param2); and Low-Hysteresis (enable below Param2, disable above Param1). Signal strength is §115§r, with checks every §12 ticks§r. Data updates every §1100 ticks§r over a §1300-second§r history window.%n%n§3§lQuest requirements§r%nObtain §11§r comparator and tick the quest after understanding the measurement target and threshold directions. The comparator is §2detected without consumption§r; the quest does not automatically check the redstone circuit or parameter settings.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAABA.name=§d§lPocket-Sized Oversight
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAABA.desc=§3§lMonitoring and binding§r%nThe first right-click binds the Portable Wireless Network Monitor to the current player. Later ordinary right-clicks cycle the HUD through Off, Normal and Scientific. Shift-right-click your own terminal to open the viewer and settings; Shift-right-click an unbound or borrowed terminal to bind ownership to yourself. The default §1H§r key opens the carried terminal viewer and can be rebound. In inventory or a Baubles slot, it displays the bound grid's EU, instantaneous and average EU/t, and power tier. This quest requires crafting §11§r monitor and ticking the confirmation.%n%n§3§lBattery installation and charging§r%nShapelessly craft the monitor with a compatible GT/IC2 rechargeable battery to install it; the item remains the same monitor. Battery capacity and voltage tier determine its buffer and charging rate, and the new battery's charge enters the buffer. While carried in inventory or a Baubles slot with a partly empty buffer, it requests a full refill every §1200 ticks (10 seconds)§r. The grid pays supplied EU × (§11§r + downlink loss fraction).%n%n§3§lTool charging and battery replacement§r%nCompatible electric tools in inventory, armor and Baubles slots charge simultaneously at §11A × battery voltage§r per tool, limited by the remaining buffer. Charge updates preserve held-tool use and mining. Shapelessly craft with a new battery to replace the installed one: the new battery retains its own charge, while the old battery receives the remaining monitor buffer and returns to inventory, or drops if inventory is full. Tips appear on first acquisition and after §110§r and §120 minutes§r of continued possession, for §13§r tips in total.%n%n§3§lCharging status and settings§r%nA battery-equipped monitor adds a charging status line: Buffer empty, Standby, Charging and Charging complete. After §11 second§r without new charging demand, Charging complete appears for about §15 seconds§r before Standby; new demand resumes Charging immediately. Use /gtswn hud charge on/off to toggle the charging line; the eu, instant and average subcommands control the energy, instantaneous and average lines. All four lines have configuration options, and commands save their corresponding settings.%n%n§3§lBattery removal and recipe lookup§r%nShapelessly craft a battery-equipped monitor with a GT5U crowbar to remove its battery. The old battery retains the remaining buffer charge and returns to inventory, or drops if inventory is full. NEI provides §12§r crafting-table examples: installing a GT small lithium battery and removing it with a GT5U crowbar. Installation and replacement accept compatible batteries beyond that example. An equipped monitor shows its buffer through GT/IC2 energy tooltips and a charge bar.%n%n§3§lBind real screens and view remotely§r%nOrdinary right-click a physical Network Info Panel with the monitor to bind it, up to §116§r screens. Configure EU Trend, AE Trend or AE Inventory on the original screen first. The viewer displays the selected screen's complete native content, retaining its number format, time window and trend colors. EU, AE Chart and AE Inventory shortcuts choose bound screens in that mode; previous/next switches screens, and unbind removes the current screen. Keep the original screen chunk loaded.%n%n§3§lSelect entries for the HUD§r%nManage HUD entries separately from the full-screen viewer. A chart's title, values and plot can each be selected; a mini chart can appear alongside the grid overview and charging status. EU energy, instantaneous rate and average rate occupy three physical lines but count as one screen group when rotating. §1[EU Trend]§r and §1[AE Trend]§r are single group headings, with no numbered [1] prefix. Each AE inventory item or fluid has independent Instant and Average checkboxes; selecting both uses one row, instant first and the §15-minute§r average second. Every inventory row starts with §1[AE Stock]§r before its item or fluid icon, then its name, stock and selected rates.%n%n§3§lLayout and toolbox charging§r%nThe HUD settings page controls position, scale, line spacing, background and rotation. A battery-equipped monitor also charges supported electric tools inside GT toolboxes, processing up to §12§r boxes every §120 ticks§r in rotation. An open toolbox GUI, its damage animation or player swinging skips that box; nested toolboxes are not traversed. The toolbox battery slot follows the existing charging path.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAABQ.name=§6§lA Connection Across Dimensions
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAABQ.desc=§3§lPurpose and quest§r%nThe Wireless Network Link Terminal installs Link Anchor covers on supported GT energy containers. Energy covers draw from the wireless grid, while power covers upload machine output. This quest requires crafting §11§r Link Terminal.%n%n§3§lModes and interaction§r%nShift-right-click the terminal to switch Energy and Power modes; its texture indicates the active mode. The default grid interaction interval is §1600 ticks (30 seconds)§r, with §115%%§r downlink loss and §10%%§r uplink loss. Configure InteractionRateTicks and the losses in config/gtswn/gtswn_network.cfg. §cShorter intervals increase interaction frequency and processing overhead.§r%n%n§3§lBuffers and operating requirements§r%nEnergy covers use a finite buffer with a default capacity of §1V × A × 800 EU§r; power covers use Long.MAX_VALUE as their buffer limit. The wireless EU grid supports cross-dimension transfer, while participating machines still need their chunks loaded.%n%n§3§lTerminal UI and finding link nodes§r%nAlt-right-click air opens the Link Terminal UI. Hold Alt+Shift+right-click air for about §11 second§r to reveal nodes in the current Energy or Power mode for §115 seconds§r; repeat within that period to show both types together. Chat reports the count. Right-click installation supplies configured covers directly; bare covers without configuration do not work and need no separate crafting step.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAABg.name=§1§lLight Up Every Corner
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAABg.desc=§3§lPreparation and use§r%nSwitch the Link Terminal to Energy mode and right-click a cover-compatible position on a supported GT base energy container. Energy mode shows a yellow §13 × 3§r guide grid. Binding a laser hatch consumes §11§r Laser Vacuum Pipe. Tick this quest after understanding the binding procedure.%n%n§3§lBuffer and power delivery§r%nThe cover injects up to §1V × A EU§r per tick and refills from the wireless grid at each interaction interval. Capacity is V × A × (interaction ticks + redundancy ticks) EU. The defaults are §1600§r interaction ticks and §1200§r redundancy ticks, giving §1V × A × 800 EU§r. Default downlink loss is §115%%§r; it and the interaction interval are configurable in config/gtswn/gtswn_network.cfg.%n%n§3§lRemoval and remaining energy§r%nWhen the cover is removed, its remaining buffer is credited to the wireless grid at (§11§r − uplink loss fraction). §cThe remainder returns to the grid rather than as a player item; keep the machine chunk loaded.§r%n%n§3§lAdjust the current§r%nShift-right-click the installed cover to open its settings. Only amperage is editable, with a minimum of §10.1 A§r; the remaining parameters are read-only. Start with a current appropriate to the machine, then check the actual buffer and grid behavior. This checkbox confirms understanding rather than checking the installed cover automatically.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAABw.name=§5§lGather Every Joule
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAABw.desc=§3§lPreparation and use§r%nShift-right-click the Link Terminal to select Power mode, then right-click a supported machine with readable energy output to install the power cover. Power mode shows a purple §13 × 3§r guide grid. Tick this quest after understanding energy upload.%n%n§3§lUpload and losses§r%nEach tick, the cover draws up to §1V × A EU§r from available machine output into its buffer, then uploads it at each grid interaction. The default interval is §1600 ticks (30 seconds)§r with §10%%§r uplink loss. Its buffer limit is Long.MAX_VALUE. Configure the interval and losses in config/gtswn/gtswn_network.cfg.%n%n§3§lOutput restrictions§r%nThe cover blocks output to physical cables on that face to prevent double counting; it draws no energy from machines without output. The machine chunk must remain loaded for collection and upload.%n%n§3§lAdjust the current§r%nShift-right-click the installed cover to open its settings. Only amperage is editable, with a minimum of §10.1 A§r; the remaining parameters are read-only. Start with a current appropriate to the machine, then check the actual buffer and grid behavior. This checkbox confirms understanding rather than checking the installed cover automatically.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAACA.name=§3§lTrends on a Wall
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAACA.desc=§3§lPurpose and quest§r%nThe Network Info Panel displays wireless EU data and real-time changes and history curves for ME items and fluids. This quest requires crafting §11§r panel. EU history is shared by the placing player's UUID; AE monitoring data is recorded separately for each panel position.%n%n§3§lPreparation and use§r%nRight-click with an empty hand to open configuration. ME monitoring requires a connection to the ME network. Right-click with an item or a recognized filled fluid container to bind a chart target or add or remove live monitoring entries, depending on the active tab. Keep the panel chunk loaded.%n%n§3§lHistory recording§r%nHistory offers §18§r nominal sampling presets: §15 minutes, 1 hour, 8 hours, 24 hours, 7 days, 1 month, 3 months and 1 year§r. Each preset retains §161§r FIFO data points. Actual coverage depends on the sampling interval and available samples; use the displayed coverage. Several curve styles are available, with Fritsch–Carlson splines as the default.%n%n§3§lThree display modes§r%nEU Trend shows grid energy and changes. AE Trend charts one bound item or fluid with a blue stock axis and an orange change-rate axis. AE Inventory lists multiple targets with icons, stock, instantaneous changes and average changes over §1retained samples§r; it supports scrolling, grid layout, font size, bold text and icon size. Choose the mode and monitoring targets on the physical panel.%n%n§3§lTake the screen with you§r%nBind the physical panel by right-clicking it with the Portable Wireless Network Monitor. H or Shift-right-click your own monitor opens the full remote screen; Manage HUD entries selects a simplified persistent display. The monitor uses the actual panel as its source, so its bindings do not replace the panel's ME connection or chunk-loading requirements. EU flat/rising/falling color settings also apply in the portable viewer and HUD mini charts.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAACQ.name=§3§lThe Bigger Picture
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAACQ.desc=§3§lPurpose and layout§r%nPlace extender panels beside compatible main or extender panels to form a continuous rectangular display. The structure finds adjacent compatible screen blocks and selects the largest filled rectangle containing the main panel. Keep the intended display area continuous and filled.%n%n§3§lQuest and reward§r%nThis quest requires crafting §12§r extender panels and awards §28 additional extender panels§r. The crafting target is not a consumption task; the reward supplies extra items rather than returning materials.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAACg.name=§d§lQuantize the ME Network
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAACg.desc=§3§lPreparation and quantization§r%nThis quest requires crafting §11§r ME Network Quantum Terminal. Right-click a formed, online controller structure supplied with AE network power. Once its proxy is ready, the connected controller structure is quantized, the terminal binds to its anchor, and channel capacity is recorded.%n%n§3§lConnections and power§r%nUse the bound terminal to place Quantum Nodes and bridge devices into the ME network. The quantized controller filters ordinary connections while retaining controllers in the same structure, energy acceptors or energy components, and Quartz Fibre facing the controller. Maintain a valid AE power path. Shift-right-click air to open the terminal interface and choose from §116§r network colors, with purple as the default. Only the central particles on the terminal itself change color; Quantum Nodes and incorporated devices bound to that network follow its color, helping distinguish multiple networks.%n%n§3§lChannel overload§r%n§cExceeding channel capacity starts a 3-minute countdown; remaining overloaded until it ends detonates the controller structure.§r Returning within capacity cancels the countdown. With AE2 infinite channels enabled, §2channel caps and overload explosions do not apply§r.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAACw.name=§d§lCompress the Quantum Ring!
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAACw.desc=§3§lPreparation and use§r%nBind the ME Quantum Terminal to a quantized controller, then right-click the target position to place a Quantum Node. It connects adjacent AE2 devices to the anchor network through a virtual bridge. Tick this quest after understanding placement and bridging.%n%n§3§lChannels and power draw§r%nEach bridge uses a dense connection carrying up to §132§r channels; the node itself consumes no channel. Default idle draw is §110 AE/t§r. §2Virtual bridging supports cross-dimension connections without ordinary cable routing distance limits§r.%n%n§3§lChunk loading and removal§r%n§cThe anchor dimension and chunk must already be loaded; Quantum Nodes do not load their anchor.§r Shift-right-click a node with the Quantum Terminal to remove it.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAADA.name=§d§lMind Your Channels
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAADA.desc=§3§lChecking channels§r%nShift-right-click air to open the Quantum Terminal interface and check used channels, total capacity, percentage, incorporated-device count and the channels those devices actually use. Press E or Esc to close the interface. The percentage uses the red overload color only when usage exceeds total capacity. Tick this quest after understanding capacity and overload handling.%n%n§3§lCapacity and warnings§r%nController capacity is exposed faces × §132§r channels; shared faces between neighboring controllers are excluded at both ends. Each node bridge carries up to §132§r channels. When a node gains channels and total usage reaches at least §195%%§r without exceeding capacity, it triggers a chat warning; remaining in that range does not continuously repeat the warning.%n%n§3§lOverload and expansion§r%n§cAn overload lasting 3 minutes detonates the entire controller structure; returning within capacity during the countdown cancels the explosion.§r With AE2 infinite channels enabled, §2there is no channel cap or overload explosion§r. Check usage after adding devices, reserve capacity before expansion, and remove idle nodes when necessary.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAADQ.name=§6§lThe Machine Ledger
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAADQ.desc=§3§lPreparation and quest§r%nThe Device Info Terminal manages supported GT machines in an EU ledger. This quest detects §11§r terminal and §2does not consume it§r. The crafting-table recipe uses §12§r LV Sensors, §12§r LV Emitters, §13§r Steel Plates, §11§r Computer Screen Cover and §11§r Ender Pearl.%n%n§3§lBinding, viewing and names§r%nWith visualization off, right-click a supported machine to bind it; supported machines placed while carrying the terminal also bind automatically. Right-click air to open the list. Rows show Running, Idle or Stopped status, actual EU input/output, instantaneous and average EU/t, position and available recipe previews. Consumption is negative; direct GT wireless-grid transactions are also measured, and icons identify this mod's input/output link nodes. Hover the name for a readable recipe, or enable Show Recipe in the list. Name, Status, Instantaneous, Average and Position can be sorted; choose Normal, Scientific, metric K/M/G/T/P or GT voltage-tier formatting. Alt-left-click a machine name to rename it, select a color and toggle bold; Ctrl-click a row unbinds it remotely.%n%n§3§lSampling and ignored machines§r%nSampling defaults to once every §110 seconds§r; instantaneous EU/t uses the latest observation of the recent §120-tick§r flow window, and averages use §160§r rolling points. Shift-right-click a machine, or Shift-click its list row, toggles tracking/ignored only for this terminal. For an unbound machine it records the preference without binding. Passive energy storage and transformers default to ignored. Ignored entries stay gray with zero readings and are not tracked; this preference does not switch the machine itself off.%n%n§3§lCharged scan§r%nHold Alt+right-click for about §11 second§r, including while aiming at a block. Charge completion immediately starts a scan of your and your team's machines in loaded chunks of the current dimension; repeating the charge does not cancel an active scan. Ordinary scans skip ignored machines. Charge again with the same terminal within §115 seconds§r after completion to force them into the list, keeping their ignored status and gray zero readings. The default binding limit is §11024§r; scans do not load chunks.%n%n§3§lPersistent visualization and settings§r%nShift+Alt+right-click toggles and saves visualization. The enabled terminal gains an enchantment glint; its bound-machine anchors remain visible while it is in inventory. Labels can independently show names, status, live EU input/output and recipes. Shift-right-click air or a non-machine opens settings for range, opacity, occlusion, device/label limits and other display options. Energy heat uses warm red for consumption and green for generation, with smooth radii weighted from instantaneous and average load. Heat size ranges from §11–1000§r, default §110§r (ten times the original radius limit); label size ranges from §11–100§r, default §110§r (150 percent of the original).%n%n§3§lTeleportation and server settings§r%nHold this terminal with visualization enabled and ordinary right-click while aiming at its bound machine or marker to teleport; when no bound target is aimed at, the list opens. The row teleport button provides the same travel action. Default cost is §11 XP level§r and cooldown §110 ticks (0.5 seconds)§r. A loaded, valid destination is required: the nearest safe position in front of or above the controller is chosen, and flying players may use clear air without ground support. XP is charged only after successful teleportation. Existing configuration is retained; singleplayer changes save to the local instance, and multiplayer OP changes on the Server tab persist to server configuration and synchronize online players. Set the XP cost to §10§r for free travel; keep client and server versions aligned.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAADg.name=§d§lQuantum Incorporation Anchor
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAADg.desc=§3§lPreparation and quest§r%nThis quest detects §11§r Quantum Incorporation Anchor and §2does not consume it§r. The LV Assembler recipe uses §11§r Basic Coated Circuit Board, §11§r LV Emitter, §11§r LV Sensor, §12§r Steel Plates, §11§r Ender Pearl and §14§r Fine Copper Wires to produce §116§r anchors in §110 seconds§r at §130 EU/t§r.%n%n§3§lIncorporation and removal§r%nFirst bind the Quantum Terminal to a quantized controller. Hold Alt while aiming at a full AE2 block or GT ME hatch/bus to preview eligibility, then Alt-right-click to incorporate it into the bound ME network, consuming §11§r anchor. Alt-right-click it again to remove incorporation without consuming another anchor; Creative mode consumes none. Cable parts such as panels and buses are unsupported. §cIncorporation hardens the device against explosions and blocks physical connections only to actual ME cables. Adjacent GT ME hatches and Quantum Nodes retain their native directional connections. Use wire cutters to toggle GT hatch all-side connections; native direction, channel-capacity and security rules still apply.§r The original device uses channels normally; the terminal interface shows incorporated-device count and actual channel use.%n%n§3§lLocating members and operation§r%nHold Alt and hold right-click in air for §11 second§r to reveal the Quantum Nodes, incorporated devices and quantized controllers configured with this terminal for §115 seconds§r. Chat confirms that only members configured with this terminal are shown. Repeat the action during that period to reveal all nearby loaded quantum members, with outlines colored by their respective network. §cKeep the anchor and member chunks loaded.§r Each effectively incorporated block adds an actual §150 AE/t§r drain from AE energy storage by default; this additional fee is unaffected by the AE power multiplier. It excludes the original device and channel power costs, which AE calculates normally. Removing incorporation or losing the connection stops this additional fee.
